ome and apologize for a lapse in  judgment and the problem tonight is that  the prime minister is essentially doing  what he's done throughout all of the  last eight months he's blaming others so  for for partygate he was blaming the  media for reporting it and people to for  listening to it and he really at the end  of the day wasn't that sorry about it  tonight he's blaming him  but what about you know what about the  downing street operation i mean there  are parallels here not with the the  allegations but parallels all the way  back it seems that the in the autumn of  last year with owen patterson gate if i  can call it that in that  people were being sent out ministers  were being sent out to say things that  they'd been told by downing street which  subsequently turned out not to be  exactly true and that really upsets  people doesn't it and that's what it  seems has led to this evening so what  about a change what about a change of  the personnel once again how many has he  had so far two or three  well this is the third iteration derma i  don't i don't think that the change of  personnel is going to do it now um we we  have a situation where cabinet ministers  are resigning and presumably others are  thinking about their position tonight  now jacob rees-mogg very loyal you know  he's going to say anything short of  losing a vote the prime minister should  stay i i can't see how changing the team  is going to make any difference to uh  backbenchers or cabinet ministers and  even if the rest of the cabinet doesn't  move against him i suspect what we'll  see is a  change in the leadership and the  constitution of the committee of the  1922 and rather than waiting six months  i suspect they'll move quickly to have a  vote of confidence which at the moment i  suspect the prime minister even deep  down if he's being honest himself knows  he's probably going to lose and that  that is why the writing is on the wall  but but i think that you know he will  take the v